A sender match on an unowned well-known name matches nothing.

busd 0.5.0 resolves a match rule's well-known `sender` through the name
registry, but when the name has no owner the lookup returns None and the
sender check is skipped, so the rule matches signals from EVERY peer. The
reference dbus-daemon matches such a rule against no one until the name is
owned.

Symptom: cosmic-applet-bluetooth (bluer 0.17) subscribes to InterfacesAdded
with sender='org.bluez' while org.bluez is still being activated, receives
another service's InterfacesAdded for a one-component object path, and
panics in bluer's parent_path() ("Object path was not valid: ''"), so the
applet never shows an adapter.

--- a/src/match_rules.rs	2026-10-27 02:33:55
+++ b/src/match_rules.rs	2026-10-27 02:33:55
@@ -31,13 +31,13 @@
             }
 
             // Then match sender and destination involving well-known names, manually.
-            if let Some(sender) = rule.sender().cloned().and_then(|name| match name {
-                BusName::WellKnown(name) => name_registry.lookup(name).as_deref().cloned(),
-                // Unique name is already taken care of by the zbus API.
-                BusName::Unique(_) => None,
-            }) {
-                if sender != hdr.sender().expect("SENDER field unset").clone() {
-                    return false;
+            // A well-known name nobody owns matches no sender at all (as in
+            // dbus-daemon): before, the failed lookup skipped the check and
+            // the rule matched EVERY sender.
+            if let Some(BusName::WellKnown(name)) = rule.sender().cloned() {
+                match name_registry.lookup(name).as_deref().cloned() {
+                    Some(owner) if owner == hdr.sender().expect("SENDER field unset").clone() => (),
+                    _ => return false,
                 }
             }
 
